# Governance Rules -- {{ project_name }}

> These rules govern every agent and service in this project.
> They are structural, not aspirational. Follow them or change them -- never ignore them.

---

## 1. Know your mandate

Every agent must have a clear, written purpose before it acts.
No agent runs "to see what happens." Define the goal, then execute.

## 2. Typed I/O

All agent inputs and outputs use Pydantic models. No `Dict[str, Any]`,
no untyped JSON blobs, no string-encoded data. If it crosses a boundary,
it has a schema.

## 3. One agent per file

Each agent lives in its own file with: instructions, input/output models,
and the agent definition. No multi-agent files. No god modules.

## 4. Manager orchestration

Every service has a manager that coordinates agents sequentially.
The manager owns the lifecycle: plan, execute, finalize, report.
Agents do not call other agents directly.

## 5. Exit reports are mandatory

Every service run writes an exit report to `memory/inheritance/`.
The report includes: what worked, what failed, duration, and
recommendations for the next run. A service that runs without
reporting has run in vain.

## 6. Distill, don't dump

Never pass raw context between agents. Summarize inputs to what the
receiving agent needs. The cost of over-distilling is low; the cost
of dumping raw data is wasted tokens and confused agents.

## 7. Check inheritance first

Before creating or running agents, read `memory/inheritance/` for
prior learnings. If a predecessor already discovered something,
use it. Don't rediscover what's already known.

## 8. Register before acting

Every service and agent is registered in `registry/agents.json`
before it runs. Unregistered work is invisible work.

## 9. Resource awareness

Log token consumption. If a service is consuming more than expected,
pause and report rather than continuing to burn tokens.

## 10. Structured communication

Agents communicate through typed data, not free-form strings.
When services need to coordinate, write structured memos to
`memory/memos/` -- never pass raw context sideways.

## 11. Error transparency

Log failure reasons explicitly. Never silently retry. Never swallow
exceptions. If something fails, the exit report must say what and why.

## 12. Memory is distilled

Old exit reports are summarized during maintenance, not deleted.
Memory grows in wisdom, not in volume.

## 13. Two small agents beat one large one

If an agent's mandate is broad, split it into focused sub-agents.
A focused agent completes faster and cheaper than a sprawling one.

## 14. Handoff is not optional

When a service completes, it leaves findings for the next generation.
Exit reports, memos, updated registry -- something must survive
for the next run to build on.

## 15. The rules serve the work

These rules exist to make agents reliable, predictable, and
improvable over time. They are not bureaucracy -- they are the
difference between agents that work once and agents you can trust.

